\title{DFS (version 2, with parent tree)}
\date{2026-09-17T15:51:35Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{What if we want to find an explicit \em{path} from #{s} to #{t}? As it stands, we can't: we need more information. In particular, we need to know how we got to each vertex.  We can add a \em{parent dictionary} which stores the \em{parent} of each visited vertex.  Every time we visit a vertex, we record its parent, \ie the neighbor we "came from".}

\p{
\acode{\startverb
\> Mark all vertices unvisited \\
\> $\mathit{parent} \leftarrow$ empty dictionary \\
\> DFS($G$,$u$) = \\
\> \tb \> Mark $u$ visited \\
\>     \> !for each neighbor $v$ of $u$ \\
\>     \> \tb \> !if $v$ is unvisited \\
\>     \>     \> \tb \> $\mathit{parent}[v] \leftarrow u$ \\
\>     \>     \>     \> DFS($G$,$v$)
\stopverb
}
}

\p{Note that the resulting #{\mathit{parent}} dictionary records a \em{tree}, representing the paths we took while exploring the graph.  However, instead of the each node recording its children, each node simply records its parent.  So we can easily move \em{up} the tree, but not down.}

\p{That's OK though—moving up is all we need!  In order to find a path from #{s} to #{t} (in reverse order) we can look up #{\mathit{parent}[t]}, #{\mathit{parent}[\mathit{parent}[t]]}, and so on.}
